dagnabit(7)

# NAME

dagnabit - concepts behind the NATO-level package layout and export facades

# DESCRIPTION

*dagnabit*(1) manages the directory structure of a Go module (or cargo workspace) so that the package dependency graph stays a clean DAG that can be read off the file tree. This page explains the model the tool enforces; *dagnabit*(1) documents the commands.

# THE LEVEL RULE

Every package (go) or crate (rust) is assigned a _height_ computed from the internal dependency graph: a package with no internal dependencies has height 0, and any other package has a height one greater than the greatest height among its dependencies (longest chain, computed with Kahn's algorithm). A dependency cycle is an error; no layout can be computed for it.

Only edges between packages under the same prefix count. An import from outside the prefix (such as _cmd/_) adds no edge, and neither does an import made only from a _\_test.go_ file: in go mode the graph is read from *go list*'s non-test *Imports*. A package with no counted edges at all, whether it is imported only by _cmd/_, only by tests, or not at all, still belongs to the graph and has height 0.

Each height maps to a NATO phonetic _level_ name, in order:

```
0 alfa bravo charlie delta echo foxtrot golf hotel india juliett kilo lima
mike november oscar papa quebec romeo sierra tango uniform victor whiskey
xray yankee zulu
```

Height 0 is the literal directory name _0_; height 1 is _alfa_, and so on. A height beyond _zulu_ (26) is an error.

The consequence is the layout invariant: in a repositioned tree, a package at a given level depends only on packages at strictly lower levels. Reading the directory name tells you where a package sits in the graph.

# LAYOUT

A package lives at _<prefix>/<level>/<leaf>_ (the default, *-depth 3*) or _<level>/<leaf>_ (*-depth 2*). The _leaf_ is the package's own directory name.

Levels are not chosen by hand. *dagnabit* recomputes them from the live import graph and moves packages whose directory does not match, rewriting imports. *dagnabit -n* (dry-run) prints a *would-move* event per misplaced package and prints nothing when the layout is already correct, which makes it usable as a drift check.

Because levels shift as dependencies change, documents refer to packages by leaf only, written _\*/<leaf>_. The dewey library convention (see _libs/dewey/CLAUDE.md_ in the purse-first repository) requires leaf names to be unique across the tree and the layout to be strictly _<branch>/<leaf>_ with no sub-packages; the dagnabit library exports validators for both (_ValidateUniqueLeaves_ and _ValidateTwoLayerLayout_), but the *dagnabit* command does not run them as a general check. Only *dagnabit rename* enforces both, as a precondition; *reposition*, *move* and *export* do not check leaf uniqueness.

# FACADES

Packages under _internal/_ are not importable from outside the module. *dagnabit export* generates thin _facade_ packages under _pkgs/_ that re-export the symbols of opted-in internal packages, giving the module a public surface that does not contain the (moving) level segment: the facade is keyed by leaf name, so repositioning an internal package does not change its public import path.

Facades are generated files, stamped with a *Code generated by dagnabit (VERSION+COMMIT); DO NOT EDIT.* header. A repository should exclude them from its own formatter and linter passes; *dagnabit export --check* verifies the committed facades match a fresh generation. In go mode a facade re-exports types as aliases and values as bindings; in rust mode it is a glob re-export crate over a _<name>\_internal_ crate. See *dagnabit*(1), *GENERATED OUTPUT*.

# INIT-SMOKE

A separate concern from layout: *dagnabit init-smoke* generates per-arch test files that blank-import every package that builds for an architecture listed in _dagnabit.toml_, so that a package *init()* that fails only on that architecture is caught when the test binary is instantiated. See *dagnabit*(1), *init-smoke*.

# IN THIS REPOSITORY

purse-first applies the model to its own _libs/dewey_ module. The conformist linters published as _lib.conformistLinters.dewey-reposition_ and _lib.conformistLinters.dewey-facade-export_ (in _nix/linters/_) run *dagnabit -n internal* and *dagnabit export --check* respectively and fail on drift; downstream dewey-layout repositories can import them.

# SEE ALSO

*dagnabit*(1), *conformist*(1)
